00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:04.610
- [Instructor] We're
told that a 0.450 gram

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:09.610
potassium supplement contains
22% potassium by mass.

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:14.196
The potassium is present in the supplement

00:00:14.196 --> 00:00:18.730
as potassium chloride,
which has a molar mass

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:23.540
of 74.55 grams per mole.

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:26.409
How many grams of
potassium chloride are in

00:00:26.409 --> 00:00:28.560
the potassium supplement?

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.670
So pause this video and
see if you can figure that

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:33.560
on your own, and I will give you a hint.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:35.490
You might need a periodic table,

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:37.420
so I'll give you one right over there.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:39.150
All right, now let's work
through this together.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:42.080
So they already give us the molar mass

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:44.600
of potassium chloride,

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:46.780
but it'll be useful for us to know

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:50.440
the molar mass of potassium itself.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:51.970
And we can see it's 39.10.

00:00:53.043 --> 00:00:53.876
So for potassium, it is 39.10.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:59.470
That's its average atomic mass,

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:03.631
but you could also view
it as 39.10 grams per mole

00:01:03.631 --> 00:01:06.960
of potassium, could write it like that.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.510
And that is, I think, all we will need

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:11.860
the periodic table of elements for.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:14.544
And so, how do we think
about how many grams

00:01:14.544 --> 00:01:18.678
of potassium chloride are
in the potassium supplement?

00:01:18.678 --> 00:01:20.297
Well what I could do is, I could say,

00:01:20.297 --> 00:01:22.590
"All right, what's the total
mass of my supplement,"

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:25.510
and multiply it by 22% to figure out

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:28.770
what is the mass of potassium I have.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:30.130
So let me figure that out.

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:34.160
So mass of potassium,

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:39.060
that is going to be my 0.450 grams

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:43.700
times 22%, which is going to be equal to,

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:45.440
I'll get my calculator out,

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:50.440
0.450 times 22%, or .22,

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:55.249
is equal to 0.099.

00:01:55.249 --> 00:01:59.140
0.099.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:02.760
So that's how many grams
of potassium I have.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.940
And now I can use that to figure out

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:07.350
how many moles of potassium I have.

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:09.530
And if I know how many
moles of potassium I have,

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:12.810
all the potassium is present
as potassium chloride.

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:14.380
Because then I'll have
the same number of moles

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.611
of potassium chloride, and
then I could figure out,

00:02:16.611 --> 00:02:18.515
well, based on.

00:02:18.515 --> 00:02:21.400
And if I know the number of
moles of potassium chloride,

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:24.030
well then I know the mass
of potassium chloride,

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:25.550
because they give us the molar mass.

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:27.300
So let's do that.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:31.703
So we have 0.099

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:35.620
grams of potassium.

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:38.920
And I wanna figure out how
many moles of potassium I have.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:41.430
So I'm gonna multiply that times,

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:45.540
one mole of potassium

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:49.400
has a mass of how many grams of potassium?

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:51.780
And we can see it right over there.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:54.780
It's 39.10 grams per mole.

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:57.143
So 39.10.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.300
One mole is equivalent to 39.10 grams,

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:02.910
if we're dealing with potassium.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:06.318
So this calculation will
tell me moles of potassium.

00:03:06.318 --> 00:03:10.340
Now, I have exactly one potassium atom

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:13.530
for every potassium chloride molecule.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:15.380
And so, whatever this number is,

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:17.260
I'm gonna have the same number of moles

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:18.600
of potassium chloride,

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.930
but just to make that clear,
I will write this down.

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:23.860
I have one mole

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:28.795
of potassium chloride for every one

00:03:28.795 --> 00:03:32.630
mole of potassium.

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:35.280
And you could already see how
the units will cancel out.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:36.968
That will cancel with that.

00:03:36.968 --> 00:03:39.080
That will cancel with that.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:41.430
And so, this calculation
is going to tell me

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:43.630
moles of potassium chloride,

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:46.030
but I wanna know the mass
of potassium chloride.

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:50.130
So then I will multiply
this by the molar mass,

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:52.640
times 74, and I'm gonna
do this in another color,

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:57.640
just makes it look nice, 74.55 grams

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:01.420
of potassium chloride

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:04.880
for every mole

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:07.240
of potassium chloride.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:09.860
And so then, this will cancel with this,

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:11.450
and this will tell me how many grams

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:14.330
of potassium chloride I actually have.

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:16.220
So let's get the calculator out again.

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:17.630
So I have 0.099

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:21.730
divided by 39.10,

00:04:21.730 --> 00:04:26.020
divided by 39.10,

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:29.380
times one, times 74.55,

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:34.380
times 74.55, is going to be equal to that.

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:38.480
And then let me think about
my significant figures

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:40.328
or my significant digits here.

00:04:40.328 --> 00:04:43.570
So let's see, out of all
of the information I used,

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:46.365
I have three significant figures here,

00:04:46.365 --> 00:04:49.662
I have three here, I have four here,

00:04:49.662 --> 00:04:52.100
I have four here.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:54.400
So I'm multiplying a bunch
of things and dividing,

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:55.670
so I have to take the minimum number

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:58.110
